OASIS 0004 -- driver card allocator: 64-bit card addresses, 1 GB aligned huge pool.
Apply on top of 0003 (git -C parcore/libstf/coyote apply 0003-... then 0004-...); only matters
for EN_MEM shells (the HBM slot bitstreams, build-124 on). With en_mem=0 the card allocator is
never called, so rev-2 bitstreams (119/125) see no change.

1. alloc_card_memory(): chunk id (uint32_t) << page_shift is a 32-bit shift, so every chunk at or
   above 2^20 (4 GB into a pool) wrapped to the bottom of the pool. 0003's 12 GB huge pool was
   therefore really 4 GB, the 5th 1 GB page aliased the 1st, and free_card_memory() freed the
   aliased ids ("freeing card memory with used=false") while the real ones stayed used: 1 GB leaked
   per process per page past the 4th, until the pool ran dry (build-124, 2026-09-23: ENOMEM on the
   5th huge page at LIBSTF_HUGE_PAGES=10). Cast to uint64_t in both pools.
2. N_SMALL_CHUNKS 256K -> 192K so the huge pool starts at exactly 1 GB. create_tlb_mapping() keeps
   only the bits above the 1 GB page shift, so a 1.25 GB base made the TLB and the driver's own
   offload/sync DMAs disagree by 256 MB and put huge page 0 on top of the regular pool.
Limit afterwards: 11 x 1 GB huge pages (free_chunks > n_pages is strict), i.e. the HBM slot page
plus LIBSTF_HUGE_PAGES <= 10.

diff --git a/driver/include/coyote_defs.h b/driver/include/coyote_defs.h
index 001e67c..4ccb099 100644
--- a/driver/include/coyote_defs.h
+++ b/driver/include/coyote_defs.h
@@ -349,7 +349,12 @@ extern bool en_hmm;
     // OASIS maps up to LIBSTF_HUGE_PAGES=10 huge pages plus one 1 GB page for the HBM
     // receive slots, so the huge pool has to hold >= 12 GB; the regular pool never needed
     // 4 GB. Layout in the U55C's 16 GB HBM: MEM_START 0.25 + small 1 + huge 12 = 13.25 GB.
-    #define N_SMALL_CHUNKS (256UL * 1024UL)             // 1 GB of regular (4 KB) pages
+    // OASIS 0004: 0.75 GB of regular pages so the huge pool starts at MEM_START + 0.75 GB = exactly
+    // 1 GB. create_tlb_mapping() keeps only the address bits above the 1 GB page shift, so a huge
+    // pool based at 1.25 GB put huge page k at [1+k, 2+k) GB in the TLB but at [1.25+k, 2.25+k) GB
+    // in the driver's own bookkeeping (offload/sync DMAs), and huge page 0 overlapped the top of the
+    // regular pool. Layout now 0.25 + 0.75 + 12 = 13 GB, every huge page 1 GB aligned.
+    #define N_SMALL_CHUNKS (192UL * 1024UL)             // 0.75 GB of regular (4 KB) pages
     #define N_LARGE_CHUNKS (12UL * 256UL * 1024UL)      // 12 GB of huge pages
 #endif
 
